<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Tibur</h1><p class="st-meta">650 BCE – 338 BCE (approx.) · capital Tibur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Latin city above the falls of the Aniene, a member of the Latin League that kept its independence as a Roman ally after 338 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tibur, modern Tivoli, stood on a ridge where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aniene">Aniene</a> river drops from the hills into the plain east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Legend claimed Greek founders, three Argive brothers including Tiburtus. Settlement goes back to the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, and by the 6th century BCE it was one of the stronger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> towns.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Tibur controlled the routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> into the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> and quarried travertine. Its great sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctuary-of-hercules-victor">Hercules Victor</a> drew traders and pilgrims. As a member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/latin-league">Latin League</a> it often acted independently of Rome, and in the 360s and 350s BCE it allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> against Rome.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tibur fought on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> side in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-war">Latin War</a> of 340–338 BCE and lost territory, but unlike most Latin towns it kept independence as a Roman ally until citizenship came after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a>. It became a resort for Roman aristocrats and the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-villa">Hadrian's Villa</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tibur.jpg" alt="Tibur" width="320" height="427"><figcaption>Image: LPLT · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tivoli_montage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>650 BCE – 338 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tibur</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Latin polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tivoli</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-494&amp;focus=tibur" aria-label="Tibur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4991 684 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tibur lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5311 940L5309 947M5311 940L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5309 947h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5311 940h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5311 940h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-494&amp;focus=tibur">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

